      "problem": "Solve the absolute value inequality $|12x - 5| > 7.$",
      "worked_solution": "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $12x - 5$ whose distance from zero is greater than $7.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-5 & <-7 & \\text{or} &  & 12x-5 & >7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-5 & <-7 &  &  & 12x-5 & >7 \\\\ 12x & <-2 &  &  & 12x & >12 \\\\ x & <-\\frac{2}{12} &  &  & x & >\\frac{12}{12} \\\\ x & <-\\frac{1}{6} &  &  & x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <-\\frac{1}{6} & \\text{or} &  & x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
